The paper investigates the exponential decay of correlations in the symmetric exclusion process on a lattice of $d$ dimensions for $d\geq 1$ without any specific symmetries or periodic boundary conditions. Under the assumption that the initial configuration converges to a non-degenerate equilibrium, we show that the decay of correlations at equilibrium occurs at a rate of $t^{-\min(1,d/2)}$. Furthermore, our findings include the asymptotic behavior of the decay of pairwise correlations in the bulk of the lattice as $t$ approaches infinity. These results are achieved by first establishing the tightness of the process's equilibrium distribution, and subsequently, the convergence of the rescaled process to a non-trivial limit that is characterized by a stochastic structure that varies with time and space. These findings extend previous outcomes for $d=1$ and provide a solution to an open problem posed by McKennon, Quadrigardo, and Varughese. Moreover, our results on the decay of pairwise correlations can be quantitatively compared with Monte Carlo simulations. 

